Modeling data with the right type.

Module two gave you classes. But a plain class is a blunt instrument. It can model anything, which means it tells the reader nothing about what kind of thing you actually have. Modern Java gives you sharper tools, each of which encodes an intent that the compiler can then enforce. Choosing the right one is half of good design.

There are really four shapes of data, and one question points you to the right one: how many valid values are there, and can they change?

If there's a fixed, known set of values, you want an enum. There are exactly seven days, three states, four suits. An enum makes that set closed and named — status dot active, rather than the string "active" or the magic number one. If it's an immutable bundle of values, you want a record. A point is just an x and a y; a money amount is just an amount and a currency. A record says "this is plain data" in a single line, and generates the boilerplate for you. If it's a closed family of variants, you want a sealed type. A payment is exactly one of card, cash, or transfer — no more. A sealed interface names that family, so the compiler knows it's complete. And if it's an open-ended thing, with identity and changing state, you want an ordinary class. A bank account has a lifecycle, it mutates, and it has no fixed set of instances. That's the general case from module two.

So why does encoding intent pay off? Because when you reach for the strongest type the data allows, the compiler starts working for you. Illegal states become unrepresentable — if suit is an enum, there is simply no way to hold an invalid suit, so you never validate it, because a bad value can't exist. The reader also learns from the type: record point tells you immutable data at a glance, and sealed interface shape tells you the variants are finite. The type documents the design. And exhaustiveness gets checked. With enums and sealed types, a switch that covers every case needs no default — and if you add a new variant later, the compiler flags every switch that forgot to handle it. That's a refactor the compiler drives for you.

The trap to avoid is modelling everything as a plain class, or worse, as a string. The common smell is a string status that's really one of five values, or an int type that's really an enum, or a mutable class for what is plainly immutable data. Each one throws away a guarantee. Now you're validating strings by hand, writing equals yourself, and hunting for the one place that set an illegal value.

So here's where this module goes. We'll take each tool in turn — enums, first as constants, then enums that carry state and behaviour; records, first the one-liner, then customising and validating them; and sealed types, for closing a family. Then the feature that makes them all sing together: pattern matching, including record destructuring and exhaustive switch. And we close on the built-in annotations that let you talk to the compiler. The through-line the whole way: let the type carry the meaning, and let the compiler enforce it.
